.\" pamsignal.conf.5 - manual page for the pamsignal configuration file
.\" The @VERSION@ is substituted by meson at build time.
.TH PAMSIGNAL.CONF 5 "" "pamsignal @VERSION@" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
pamsignal.conf \- configuration file for the pamsignal PAM authentication monitor
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pamsignal
reads an optional INI-style configuration file to control brute-force
detection parameters and to define one or more alert channels (Telegram,
Slack, Microsoft Teams, WhatsApp, Discord, or a generic HTTPS webhook).

All keys are optional. If the file is absent or a key is omitted, built-in
sane defaults are used. The file may contain sensitive credentials (bot
tokens, webhook URLs with embedded secrets, bearer tokens, etc.) and should
therefore be readable only by root and the
.B pamsignal
system user.

The daemon refuses to start if the configuration file exists but is invalid
(bad syntax, unknown keys, malformed token/URL values, or mTLS paths that
fail ownership or permission checks).
.SH FILE FORMAT
The file uses a simple
.B key = value
syntax. Comments begin with
.B #
and continue to the end of the line. Whitespace around the
.B =
sign is ignored. Values are not quoted.

Example locations (depends on
.B prefix
and
.B sysconfdir
at build time):
.PP
.RS
/etc/pamsignal/pamsignal.conf
.br
/usr/local/etc/pamsignal/pamsignal.conf
.RE
.SH BRUTE-FORCE DETECTION
These four keys tune the in-memory sliding-window tracker.

.TP
.B fail_threshold
Number of failed authentication attempts (per IP or per local actor for
sudo/su) within the window before a brute-force alert is generated.
Range: 1\(en10000. Default: 5.

.TP
.B fail_window_sec
Length of the observation window in seconds. Attempts older than this
window are discarded. Range: 1\(en86400. Default: 300 (5 minutes).

.TP
.B max_tracked_ips
Maximum number of distinct source IPs (or local actors) that are tracked
simultaneously. When the table is full the oldest entry is evicted.
Range: 1\(en100000. Default: 256.

.TP
.B alert_cooldown_sec
Minimum number of seconds that must elapse between brute-force alerts for
the same key. 0 disables the cooldown. Range: 0\(en86400. Default: 60.

.SH LOGIN-AFTER-FAILURES DETECTION
.TP
.B success_after_fail_threshold
A successful login from a source IP that has produced at least this many
failed attempts, the most recent within
.B fail_window_sec
seconds, is reported as a
.B LOGIN_AFTER_FAILURES
alert (severity 9): the pattern of a guessed password. The run of failures
is cleared by any successful login from that IP, so one suspicious login
produces exactly one alert. The alert is not muted by
.BR trusted_sources ;
repeat chat alerts for the same IP are spaced by
.BR alert_cooldown_sec .
0 disables the detection. Range: 0\(en10000. Default: 3.

.SH TRUSTED SOURCES
.TP
.B trusted_sources
Comma-separated list of at most 32 IPv4 or IPv6 addresses or CIDR networks
(for example an office range, a VPN, a bastion host). The routine per-event
chat alerts
.RB ( login_success ", " login_failed )
are not sent for events whose source IP falls inside one of them.
Brute-force and login-after-failures alerts still fire for trusted sources,
and the journal trail records every event regardless.

A bare address is a single host. The prefix length must be at least 1;
hostnames are not resolved. An IPv4-mapped IPv6 peer address
.RI ( ::ffff:a.b.c.d )
is matched against the IPv4 entries. A malformed list is a hard error.
Session events carry no source IP and are unaffected; use
.B enable_notification_type
to mute them. Default: empty.

Example:
.EX
trusted_sources = 10.0.0.0/8, 203.0.113.7, 2001:db8::/32
.EE

.SH NOTIFICATION-TYPE FILTER
.TP
.B enable_notification_type
Comma-separated list of event categories that should produce chat alerts.
The journal trail
.RB ( journalctl\ \-t\ pamsignal )
always records every event; this filter only affects Telegram, Slack,
Teams, WhatsApp, Discord and custom-webhook delivery.

Valid tokens:
.BR login_success , " " login_failed , " " session_open , " " session_close , " " brute_force , " " login_after_failures , " " all .

Unknown tokens, empty values and empty list elements are hard errors.
Default (or the token
.B all
) means every category. An explicit list must name
.B login_after_failures
to receive that alert.

The existing per-event suppression for sudo/su
.B LOGIN_FAILED
(only the aggregate brute-force alert fires) is applied after this filter.

Example:
.EX
enable_notification_type = login_success,brute_force
.EE
.SH ALERT CHANNELS
Only the channels for which credentials are present are used. Multiple
channels may be configured simultaneously; each receives an independent
copy of every alert that passes the filter.

.TP
.B telegram_bot_token
Telegram bot token obtained from @BotFather. Must be of the form
.IR NNNN:alphanumeric+hyphen/underscore .

.TP
.B telegram_chat_id
Numeric chat ID or @channelname.

.TP
.B slack_webhook_url
Incoming webhook URL (https://...).

.TP
.B teams_webhook_url
Incoming webhook URL for Microsoft Teams.

.TP
.B whatsapp_access_token
Meta Cloud API access token.

.TP
.B whatsapp_phone_number_id
Phone Number ID (digits only).

.TP
.B whatsapp_recipient
Recipient phone number in international format (digits only).

.TP
.B discord_webhook_url
Discord incoming webhook URL.

.TP
.B webhook_url
Generic HTTPS endpoint that receives a JSON POST (Elastic Common Schema
plus a small pamsignal.* extension object). See
.BR pamsignal (8)
and the
.I docs/alerts.md
file in the source tree for the exact payload shape.

.SH CUSTOM WEBHOOK AUTHENTICATION (OPTIONAL)
Two independent mechanisms may be used with
.BR webhook_url .

.TP
.B webhook_auth_header
Single HTTP header in
.B "Name: value"
form (e.g.
.IR "Authorization: Bearer XXX" ).
The value is written into the curl
.B \-K
config file; it never appears on the command line. Control characters,
.BR \(dq , " and " \\ 
are rejected at load time.

.TP
.B webhook_client_cert
.TP
.B webhook_client_key
.TP
.B webhook_ca_bundle
Paths to PEM files for optional mutual-TLS client authentication.
.B webhook_client_cert
and
.B webhook_client_key
must both be present or both absent. The private key file must not be
group- or world-readable; the daemon refuses to start otherwise. All
three paths are opened with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
and must be regular files owned by root or the daemon user.

.SH RELOAD
Send
.B SIGHUP
(or run
.B systemctl reload pamsignal
) to reload the file. The new configuration is parsed into a temporary
structure; only on success is it swapped in. On any error the previous
configuration (and the current brute-force tracking table) is retained
and a warning is logged.
.SH SECURITY NOTES
The configuration file is the only place where alert credentials live.
They are never logged and are passed to the
.B curl (1)
child via a memfd-backed
.B \-K
file rather than via argv, so they do not appear in
.B /proc/<pid>/cmdline
or in process accounting.

All path values supplied for mTLS are validated at load time for
symlink attacks, regular-file status, ownership and (for the private key)
permissions. Strings that could break the curl config parser or produce
shell metacharacters are rejected early.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/pamsignal/pamsignal.conf
Default location of the configuration file (exact path depends on the
.B sysconfdir
used at build time).
.TP
.I /etc/pamsignal/pamsignal.conf.example
Example file shipped in the source tree and in some distribution packages.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pamsignal (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
The source repository contains
.B docs/threat-model.md
and
.B docs/alerts.md
which expand on the design rationale and on the exact JSON payloads
delivered to custom webhooks.
.SH AUTHOR
Tuan Nguyen <anhtuank7c@hotmail.com>